  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Blustery and colder rain showers/elevation \nwintry mix into tonight.\n\nHighest elevation METARs (Johnstown & Somerset) are reporting\nsnow. Webcams also confirm snow falling across the Laurel\nHighlands. A light coating already on the grassy areas on \nLaurel Summit near Hidden Valley. Not so in Happy Valley where \nthe sun just poked through for a minute or two. Back in the \nclouds and rain, now, though. Large scale forcing will start to\nwane as we near sunset. Then, it's mesoscale precip off the\nlakes with an assist from the upslope. Temps have begun to fall,\nand will not rise more than another deg or two in the east\nbefore they, too, have the bottom drop out. However, with all\nthe clouds and a continuous wind, we'll likely not see more \nthan 5-6F dip from sunset to sunrise. \n\nTemps do get below freezing for the west and far nrn highlands\nlater today and accumulations are on order for anywhere above\n1800ft or so - where it does precipitate overnight. So, we've\nadded a thin coating for accumulations to those areas, and\nnudged the totals up to 2 inches on the very highest hill tops\nof the Laurels. Roads, and especially bridges, will likely get \nbelow freezing there, too, with many hours (now through the \nmorning) below freezing air temps. \n\n----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Next chance for rain arrives Sun night into Mon\n\nMedium range guidance suggests an East Coast ridge will develop\nout ahead of a digging trough coming down from the Northern\nRockies into the Great Plains on Sunday. This should promote a\nbrief window of clearing skies early Saturday night, but then\nstratus becomes possible with an approaching warm front in the\nwest, and patchy valley fog will be possible to the east of the\narea with stratus. Sunday looks to be a warmer day compared to \nSaturday, but clouds will continue to increase through the day \nahead of the approaching trough. By early Sunday evening, rain \nwill likely be moving into the western highlands, then spreading\neastward through Sunday night. Model consensus takes the sfc \nlow through the eastern Great Lakes with a sweeping cold front \nmoving through the area on Monday, and potential for lake effect\nrain/snow showers later in the day Monday. Most likely rainfall\nis in the 0.25-1.00 inch range. Approx 50 pct of the membership\nof the NBM suggests wind advisory criteria (45+ mph gusts) will\nbe met in the Laurel Highlands with westerly wind gusts along \nand behind the cold front on Monday.",
